.TH CJSON_GET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_get \- navigate to a DOM node via a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

cjson cjson_get(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a call to _cjson_get(root, path).

path is a dot-separated sequence of component strings.  A component
that begins with '#' followed by one or more decimal digits addresses
an array element by zero-based index when the current node is
CJSON_LIST; otherwise the component is used as a literal object key
(including the '#' prefix).  A bare '#' with no trailing digits is
always treated as an error and causes NULL to be returned.

An empty path string returns root unchanged.

Escape sequences in path components:
  \e.   -> a literal '.' in the key (not treated as a path separator)
  \e\e   -> a literal '\e' in the key
A '\e' before any other character is left unchanged.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cjson node (typically CJSON_DICTIONARY or CJSON_LIST).
path   Dot-separated path string, e.g. "users.#0.address.city".
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Non-owning cjson handle to the target node, or NULL if any component
along the path is missing or the current node type does not support
the requested traversal.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The returned handle becomes invalid if the tree is mutated or destroyed.
Do not free the returned handle.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson city = cjson_get(doc, "users.#0.address.city");
if (cjson_type(city) == CJSON_STRING)
    printf("%s\en", cjson_str_val(city));

/* '#0' in an object context is a literal key, not an index */
cjson v = cjson_get(doc, "#0");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR _cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_type (3)
